startSummary:
This is a recorded session of about 57 minutes. Lauren Tan explains how she moved from watching every agent action to letting agents land changes that she reviews after the fact. An on-screen caption labels her a software engineer at SpaceXAI. In speech, she says she joined Cursor about five months earlier and that she works at an AI lab. We report both and do not resolve her role or employer. Her central idea is trust, which she says is earned in three ways:
- agents that verify their own work by running the real application;
- skills: written instructions refined by watching agent failures, tested with evals and packaged in her pstack plugin;
- a codebase whose architecture and CI make the easiest change also the correct one: the Dune architecture behind Grok Bot.
A host asks questions throughout, and the session ends on token cost and on non-engineers contributing. This guide separates what was said and shown from our interpretation. It maps each idea to operations practice you already know. It lets you break a deterministic model of an agent fleet. It ends with an offline lab that rehearses the same controls in a scratch repository. The transcript is a machine transcript with reviewed corrections v1 and is not human-certified.

practitioner:
Operations translation for Soyspray: signals are your queue. Each task needs:
- a claim, so it is not done twice;
- an isolated worktree;
- a check that runs the real thing;
- a small reviewable PR;
- the existing digest promotion PR as the only path to the cluster.
Agents never hold cluster credentials. If you ever adopt auto-merge, apply it to source PRs behind hard checks, never to promotion.
